the field   'd131dd02c5e6eec4693d9a0698aff95'   (31 characters, described as '32 hex')

1. AS A NUMBER -- int(s, 16)
   accepted, value 0xd131dd02c5e6eec4693d9a0698aff95
   A number has no width, so 31 digits is not short, it is just smaller.
   Nothing is missing from a number's point of view. Notice what the
   round trip does: it comes back with a LEADING ZERO restored in a
   place the sender never had one -- 0d131dd02c5e6eec4693d9a0698aff95
   That is the corruption, and it looks like a repair.

2. AS BYTES -- bytes.fromhex(s)
   ValueError -- refused, before a single byte is built
   The width IS the data here, and 31 does not divide by 2, so Python
   refuses. This is the reading you want: it is the only one of the
   three that knows a hash is 16 bytes rather than a large integer.

3. AS BYTES, THE SHELL'S WAY -- xxd -r -p
   Silently drops the last nibble and exits 0. The page's own one-liner
   shows it: printf '123' | xxd -r -p | xxd -p prints 12.
   Fifteen and a half bytes are not representable, so it writes fifteen
   and says nothing. A pipeline reading its status sees success.

WHERE THE CHECK GOES
   At the boundary, before the value becomes an object -- the same place
   every decode belongs. Two lines, and they are not the same check:
       if len(field) != 32: reject      # the width, which is the contract
       raw = bytes.fromhex(field)       # the alphabet, which is the parser's
   The second one alone would pass a 30-character field. The first alone
   would pass 32 characters of Arabic-Indic digits, which int() accepts
   and which are not hex at all: int('\u0664\u0661', 16) == 65.
